42 MODULE 0 SUMMARY: SHELL, PERMISSIONS, GIT & SSH
====================================================================

1. TERMINAL, SHELL & BASH
- Terminal: The visual window where we type commands.
- Shell: The command-line interpreter that translates human commands
  into actions for the operating system.
- Bash: The specific shell program running on Linux.
- Exit terminal: Type 'exit' or press Ctrl + D.
- Help manual: Add '--help' after a command (e.g., pwd --help).

2. KEY NAVIGATION SYMBOLS
- /   : Root directory (top of the file system) or path separator.
- ~   : Home directory shortcut (/home/cargutie).
- .   : Current working directory ("right here").
- ..  : Parent directory ("one folder up/back").
- -   : Previous directory when used with cd (cd -), or command flag.
- >   : Redirects output into a file (e.g., echo "Z" > z).
- &&  : Runs the second command only if the first one succeeds.

3. ESSENTIAL UNIX COMMANDS
- pwd   : Print Working Directory (shows current path).
- ls    : List files and folders (ls -l for details, ls -la for hidden).
- cd    : Change Directory (cd /, cd ~, cd .., cd folder_name).
- mkdir : Make Directory (create a new folder).
- rmdir : Remove empty directory.
- mv    : Move or rename a file/folder (mv old_name new_name).
- cp    : Copy a file/folder.
- cat   : Display file contents (cat -e shows $ at end of lines).
- echo  : Print text or expand variables/paths onto the screen.
- nano  : Terminal text editor (Ctrl+O to save, Ctrl+X to exit).

4. FILE PERMISSIONS (ls -l -> e.g., -rw-r--r-- / drwxr-xr-x)
- 1st char : File type ('-' = regular file, 'd' = directory).
- Next 3   : Owner permissions (User).
- Next 3   : Group permissions.
- Last 3   : Others permissions (everyone else).
- Letters  : r = Read, w = Write, x = Execute, - = Denied.

5. GIT WORKFLOW (VERSION CONTROL)
- Working Directory : Local folder where files are created/edited.
- Staging Area      : Preparation zone before saving a snapshot.
- git init -b main  : Initialize a new local Git repository.
- git status        : Check untracked, staged, or modified files.
- git add <file>    : Put file into the staging area.
- git commit -m ""  : Save a permanent snapshot (commit) locally.
- git push          : Upload local commits to GitHub (origin).
- git pull          : Download updates from GitHub to local machine.
- git log --oneline : View history of commits.
- git mv            : Rename/move a file tracked by Git.

6. SSH KEYS (SECURE AUTHENTICATION)
- One SSH key pair in ~/.ssh/ links this terminal to GitHub for ALL
  repositories (no passwords required).
- id_ed25519     : Private key (SECRET - never share or upload).
- id_ed25519.pub : Public key (safe to share and add to GitHub).
- Test connection: ssh -T git@github.com
